      "content": "You are touching upon the concept of linguistic prejudice, where societal biases deeply influence perception; juries are indeed susceptible to judging credibility based on accent or dialect. The heightened formality of Witness A can lead to an association with higher education and perceived competence, while Witness B’s colloquialism might trigger subconscious negative stereotypes regarding intelligence or attention to detail. Crucially, these social biases often supersede linguistic accuracy itself, meaning the dialect carries a powerful, potentially damaging, social weight in the courtroom dynamic.",
